===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|document-themed}}
From {{inh|en|enm|bille}}, from {{der|en|xno|bille|}}, from {{der|en|fro|bulle|}}, from {{der|en|la-med|bulla||seal, sealed document}}. {{dbt|en|bull#Etymology 2|t1=papal bull; bubble|bulla}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A written list or inventory. {{n-g|(Now obsolete except in specific senses or set phrases; [[bill of lading]], [[bill of goods]], etc.)}}
# A document, originally sealed; a formal statement or official memorandum. {{n-g|(Now obsolete except with certain qualifying words; [[bill of health]], [[bill of sale]] etc.)}}
# {{senseid|en|draft law}} A [[draft]] of a [[law]], [[present]]ed to a [[legislature]] for [[enactment]]; a [[propose]]d or [[project]]ed law.
#: {{syn|en|measure}}
#: {{ux|en|Mr. Speaker, on behalf of the Prime Minister, I beg to introduce a '''bill''' entitled{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|II|I|line=28|text=Why, I'll exhibit a '''bill''' in the parliament for the putting down of men.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-12-14| author=w:Simon Jenkins| title=We mustn't overreact to North Korea boys' toys| volume=188| issue=2| page=23| magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/2012/dec/14/north-korea-boys-toys-threat-reaction| passage=David Cameron insists that his latest communications data '''bill''' is “vital to counter terrorism”. Yet terror is mayhem. It is no threat to freedom. That threat is from counter-terror, from ministers capitulating to securocrats.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013204838/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2012/dec/14/north-korea-boys-toys-threat-reaction|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|law}} A [[declaration]] made in writing, stating some [[wrong]] the [[complainant]] has [[suffer]]ed from the [[defendant]], or a [[fault]] [[commit]]ted by some person against a law.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|text=... the legion of '''bills''' in the suit have been transformed into mere bills of mortality ...|1}}
# {{senseid|en|banknote}}{{lb|en|US|Canada}} A piece of paper [[money]]; a [[banknote]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1830|author=Anon|title=The Galaxy of Wit: Or, Laughing Philosopher, Being a Collection of Choice Anecdotes, Many of Which Originated in or about "The Literary Emporium"|passage=He gave the change for a three dollar '''bill'''. Upon examination, the '''bill''' proved to be counterfeit.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|year_published=19 March 2014|title=Cabins in the Laurel|page=231|isbn= 9781469620770|publisher=University of North Carolina Press|url=https://www.google.co.nz/books/edition/Cabins_in_the_Laurel/4WSVAwAAQBAJ|passage={{...}}So I wropped 'em up in a five dollar '''bill''' and tied 'em up and sent 'em, and they ain't back yet.”}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1970|title=w:Friend of the Devil|artist=w:Grateful Dead|passage=I ran into the Devil, babe, he loaned me 20 '''bills'''.}}
## {{lb|en|slang|North America}} One [[hundred]] [[dollar]]s.
##* {{quote-book|en|title=On the Waterfront|passage=There was no excuse, simply no excuse for not making four or five '''bills''' a week. A little initiative, that's all.|year=1954|author=w:Budd Schulberg|page=25|publisher=w:Random House}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title={{w|Skin Tight (novel)|Skin Tight}}|passage=All we got from her was Stranahan's location, and barely that. A house in the bay, she said. A house with a windmill. Easiest five '''bills''' that woman ever made.|year=1989|author=w:Carl Hiaasen|page=113|isbn=9781101436639|publisher=w:Penguin Group}}
# {{lb|en|slang|UK}} One [[hundred]] [[pound sterling|pounds sterling]].
#* '''2023''', BBC News: "Newport: Drugs gang jailed for exploiting vulnerable child" [https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-wales-66570256]
#*: In the conversation Henshall says he {{sic}} "struggling to find people to go up the roads" explaining how it would be "no good for black people" and how they need a "young white boy to go up there".<br>Stock agrees, saying how he knows "this kid" who "owes me 12 '''bills'''".
# {{senseid|en|invoice}} A written note of goods sold, [[service]]s [[render]]ed, or work done, with the price or [[charge]] owing; an [[invoice]].
#: {{ux|en|He received a '''bill''' of £9 for the groceries.}}
#: {{syn|en|account|invoice}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|III|IV|line=85|text=My lord, here is my '''bill'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|receipt}} {{lb|en|slang|India}} A written note of goods sold, [[service]]s [[render]]ed, or work done, listing the price or [[charge]] paid; a [[receipt]].
# {{senseid|en|advertisement}} A paper, written or printed, and [[post]]ed up or given away, to advertise something, as a lecture, a play, or the sale of goods
#: {{syn|en|broadsheet|broadside|card|circular|flier|flyer|handbill|poster|posting| placard| notice|throwaway}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|I|II|line=104|text=In the meantime I will draw a '''bill''' of properties, such as our play wants.}}
# A [[writing]] that [[bind]]s the [[signer]] or signers to pay a [[certain]] [[sum]] at a [[future]] day or on [[demand]], with or without [[interest]], as may be [[state]]d in the [[document]]; a [[bill of exchange]]. In the United States, it is usually called a [[note]], a note of hand, or a [[promissory note]].
#: {{syn|en|bank bill|banker's bill|bank note|banknote|Federal Reserve note|government note|greenback|note}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|I|I|line=8|text=Ay, and Rato-lorum too; and a gentleman born, Master Parson; who writes himself Armigero, in any '''bill''', warrant, quittance, or obligation, Armigero.}}
# A set of items presented together.
#: {{ux|en|We've got a brilliant new comedian on the '''bill''' tonight.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=June 26, 2017|author=Alexis Petridis| title=Glastonbury 2017 verdict: Radiohead, Foo Fighters, Lorde, Stormzy and more| url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2017/jun/26/glastonbury-2017-verdict-radiohead-foo-fighters-lorde-stormzy-and-more| passage=Meanwhile, the '''bills''' on the main stages skewed towards mainstream pop, with mixed results. Lorde’s Friday evening Other stage appearance was one of the weekend’s highlights. The staging and choreography were fantastic&nbsp;– a giant glass tank on a hydraulic platform, in and around which a troupe of dancers acted out the highs and lows of a teenage party|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261012112015/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2017/jun/26/glastonbury-2017-verdict-radiohead-foo-fighters-lorde-stormzy-and-more|archivedate=12 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|UK|{{w|Eton College}}}} A [[list]] of [[pupil]]s to be [[discipline]]d for [[break]]ing [[school]] [[rule]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|author=Sir H. C. Maxwell Lyte|title=A History of Eton College, 1440-1875|page=373
|passage=One of the best stories of the period describes the misadventure of a batch of candidates for confirmation whose names were by accident sent up to the Head-Master on a piece of paper identical in size and shape with the "'''bill'''" used by the Masters for the purpose of reporting delinquents. Keate, we are told, insisted on flogging all the boys mentioned in the document {{...}}}}

